\section{Retention window}{


The feed holds at least 60 days of history and is refreshed daily. It is a
static file with no server-side query interface, so \code{since}, \code{collection}
and \code{reason} filter the retrieved feed rather than the request. Asking for
a \code{since} date earlier than the feed reaches will therefore return a
truncated result, and a warning is issued when that happens.

There is no way to obtain older changes from this API. Use
\code{dlx_list_documents()} and the sitemap's modification dates for anything
beyond the window.
}

\section{Identifying the collection}{


The feed itself does not record which collection a document belongs to; it
reports only \code{published_in}, the publication channel. danlex derives
\code{collection}, \code{year} and \code{number} from the ELI identifier, which makes it
possible to separate legislation (\code{lta}, \code{ltb}, \code{ltc}, \code{mt}) from
administrative decisions (\code{retsinfo}) and parliamentary material (\code{ft}).

The two are related but not equivalent: \code{published_in} distinguishes
Lovtidende A, B and C from Retsinformation's own series, whereas
\code{collection} follows the ELI URI structure.
}

